
  EXTENDING THE PRIME GAP CHORD THEORY

======================================================================

  I. THE CHORD AS A CAYLEY COMPOSITION
  ----------------------------------------

  CHORD(g) = prod f(p-2) over odd primes p dividing g.
  f(n) = (n+1)/n is our superparticular homomorphism.
  f(a(+)b) = f(a)*f(b) where (+) is rapidity composition.

  So: CHORD(g) = f(c) where c = rapidity composition of
  all (p-2) values for odd primes p dividing g.

  For g = 3*5*7 = 105:
  Factors give p-2 values: 1, 3, 5.
  Rapidity composition: 1 (+) 3 (+) 5.
  1 (+) 3 = 1*3/(1+3+1) = 3/5.
  3/5 (+) 5 = (3/5)*5/(3/5+5+1) = 3/(3/5+6) = 3/(33/5) = 15/33 = 5/11.
  1(+)3 = 3/5
  3/5(+)5 = 5/11
  f(5/11) = 16/5 = 16/5
  CHORD(105) = 3.2000
  f(5/11) = 16/11? No... f(n) = (n+1)/n. f(5/11) = (5/11+1)/(5/11) = (16/11)/(5/11) = 16/5.
  CHORD(105) = 16/5 = 3.2000. CHECK: 16/5 = 3.2.

  The composed rapidity velocity for g=105 is 5/11.
  Q(5/11) = (1+5/11)/(1-5/11) = (16/11)/(6/11) = 16/6 = 8/3.
  Q(5/11) = 8/3 = 8/3

  And CHORD(105) = f(5/11) = (5/11+1)/(5/11) = 16/5.
  Hmm, f is defined for integers. Let me reconsider.

  Actually: CHORD(g) = prod f(p_i - 2).
  And f(a)*f(b) = f(a(+)b) by our homomorphism theorem.
  So CHORD = f(combined), where combined = rapidity composition of all (p_i-2).

  For g=105: combined = 1(+)3(+)5 = 5/11.
  f(5/11) = (5/11+1)/(5/11) = 16/5. Not quite right since f is for integers.

  EXTEND f to rationals: f(x) = (x+1)/x = 1 + 1/x. Works for all x != 0.
  Then f(5/11) = 1 + 11/5 = 16/5. And CHORD(105) = 16/5. MATCHES.

  So the EXTENDED homomorphism f(x) = 1 + 1/x works for rational x too!
  And the rapidity composition (+) extends to rationals too.
  The CHORD of any g is f of the rapidity composition of all (p-2).


