  IX. THE GAP DISTRIBUTION AS A FOURIER SERIES
  ----------------------------------------

  CHORD(g) is a MULTIPLICATIVE function of g.
  Multiplicative functions have Dirichlet series:

  sum_{g=1}^inf CHORD(g) / g^s = prod_{p odd} (1 + (p-1)/((p-2)*p^s))

  This product converges for Re(s) > 1.
  It is an EULER PRODUCT, like the Riemann zeta function!

  The ratio CHORD(g)/g^s at each g is the 'weight' of gap 2g
  in the Dirichlet series. The Euler product decomposes it
  into contributions from each prime.

  Compare: zeta(s) = prod (1-p^{-s})^{-1}.
  Our CHORD series: prod (1 + c_p * p^{-s}) where c_p = (p-1)/(p-2).

  The zeta function encodes the DISTRIBUTION OF INTEGERS.
  Our CHORD series encodes the DISTRIBUTION OF PRIME GAPS.
  Both are Euler products. Both decompose over primes.
  Both have the same analytic structure.

  The CHORD series is to prime gaps what zeta is to integers.


  X. THE CHORD ZETA FUNCTION
  ----------------------------------------

  Define: Z_CHORD(s) = sum_{g=1}^inf CHORD(g) * g^{-s}
  = prod_{p>=3} (1 + ((p-1)/(p-2)) * p^{-s} + ((p-1)/(p-2)) * p^{-2s} + ...)
  = prod_{p>=3} 1 / (1 - ((p-1)/(p-2)) * p^{-s})
  Wait, CHORD is multiplicative but not completely multiplicative.
  CHORD(p^k) = (p-1)/(p-2) for all k >= 1 (since the factor depends
  only on whether p divides g, not how many times).

  So: Z_CHORD(s) = prod_{p>=3} (1 + ((p-1)/(p-2)) * (p^{-s} + p^{-2s} + ...))
  = prod_{p>=3} (1 + ((p-1)/(p-2)) * p^{-s} / (1 - p^{-s}))
  = prod_{p>=3} (1 + ((p-1)/(p-2)) * 1/(p^s - 1))

  Z_CHORD(2) = 1.3976723683
  Compare: zeta(2) = pi^2/6 = 1.6449340668
  Ratio: 0.8496829122

  Z_CHORD(1.5) = 2.1097744997


  XI. THE GOLDEN RATIO IN THE CHORD STRUCTURE
  ----------------------------------------

  The chord for g with just one odd prime factor p:
  CHORD = (p-1)/(p-2) = f(p-2).

  The chord RAPIDITY = arctanh(1/(2p-3)).
  = arctanh of the velocity corresponding to the interval.

  For p=3: arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)/2 = 0.3466.
  For p=5: arctanh(1/7) = 0.1438.
  For p=7: arctanh(1/11) = 0.0911.

  The RATIO of consecutive chord rapidities:
  arctanh(1/3) / arctanh(1/7) = 2.409421
  arctanh(1/7) / arctanh(1/11) = 1.577883
  arctanh(1/11) / arctanh(1/19) = 1.730454
  arctanh(1/19) / arctanh(1/23) = 1.210882

  The ratios: 2.409, 1.578, 1.730, 1.211
  They approach ~1.5-1.6. Not phi, but in that neighborhood.

  For large p: arctanh(1/(2p-3)) ~ 1/(2p-3) ~ 1/(2p).
  Ratio for consecutive primes p, q: ~ p/q.
  For twin primes: ratio ~ 1. For distant primes: ratio ~ p/q.

  The chord rapidities decay as 1/(2p) for large p.
  This is the HARMONIC SERIES of the prime chord structure.
  Each prime adds a diminishing contribution, like the overtones
  of a sound getting quieter as the frequency increases.


  XII. THE COMPLETE PICTURE
  ----------------------------------------

  Prime gap distribution = the formal group f(n) = (n+1)/n
  evaluated at shifted primes (p-2), composed multiplicatively.

  This connects:
  1. Number theory: Hardy-Littlewood, Bateman-Horn, prime k-tuples
  2. Music: superparticular intervals, chords, consonance hierarchy
  3. Algebra: the formal group homomorphism f(a(+)b) = f(a)*f(b)
  4. Analysis: the chord zeta function (Euler product over primes)
  5. Tournaments: the same f that governs H-value achievability
  6. Physics: rapidity composition = relativistic velocity addition

  The prime gap formula pi_{2g}(N) ~ C_2 * CHORD(g) * Li_2(N)
  is a statement about number theory
  expressed in the language of music
  using the algebra of the formal group
  with the analytic structure of an Euler product
  on the same rapidity line where tournaments live.

  And the twin prime constant C_2 = 2/E[CHORD]
  = two octaves divided by the average consonance of all gaps.
  If twin primes are infinite (as conjectured),
  it's because the average consonance is finite:
  there's enough harmonic energy in the gap distribution
  to sustain the baseline frequency forever.
